   "content": [
    {
     "text": "The effective use of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) to enable interoperability in public procurement is an area of great significance for achieving the Lisbon objectives, i.e. to become the most competitive and dynamic knowledge-based economy in the world (EC 2005). However, the current European public eProcurement infrastructure is inappropriate because it is characterized by a high market fragmentation and a lack of interoperability. Governments are the largest buyer in the European Union which purchase at a level of approximately 15-20% of GDP (Ministerial_Declaration 2005).",
     "role": "background"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "M1:T3",
   "speaker": "Analyst",
   "act": "ask_topic",
   "guide": "Analyst moves the discussion to the topic 'major features, requirements and quality attributes' and asks about it."
